    "explanation": "Both of you agree on what happened, but the record cannot yet turn it into money, so this case goes to a human reviewer. The disputed amount (SGD 4.50) is held — not collected and not refunded — and no driver compensation issues.\n\nWHAT IS ESTABLISHED. The GPS trace (EV-GPS-02838) is clean for policy purposes under POL-RD-07: 104 fixes at a 10-second sample interval, 100.0% coverage, no gaps, max gap 0.0 s, so nothing is measured on a straight-line reconstruction. It records 13.2 km against a booked optimal of 10.0 km (EV-TRIP-02838): an excess of 3.2 km / 32.0%. That passes the POL-RD-01 gate (more than 20% and more than 0.8 km) but is not a MATERIAL deviation (under 50% and under 8.0 km), so the POL-RD-06 materiality check is not triggered — and no repeat pattern exists anyway (deviation_flags_90d 1 against 207 completed trips in 90 days, a ratio of 0.005; both below the 3-flag and 0.05 tests), so no driver-conduct referral fires.\n\nWHY THE MONEY CANNOT BE COMPUTED. The destination was moved during the trip. The rider's own message at 14:06:43 says 'changed the pin to the other building' (EV-CHAT-02838), and the trip record shows the dropoff amended at 14:07:23 from the original pin at 1.3563,103.8545 to 1.3591,103.8522 (EV-TRIP-02838). POL-RD-05 then requires the deviation to be re-measured against the optimal route between the AMENDED endpoints, and that figure must be read from trip_record.amended_optimal_distance_km. It is null, even though dropoff_amended_at is not. POL-RD-05 says in terms that the clause cannot then be run and that POL-RD-10(5) applies, and POL-RD-10(5) names this exact situation — a POL-RD-05 amendment with amended_optimal_distance_km null — as a mandatory escalation. I may not invent a substitute figure, and I may not fall back on the booked 10.0 km baseline, because that is the baseline the amendment displaced.\n\nTRIGGER AND WHAT WOULD RESOLVE IT. Trigger: POL-RD-10(5). The missing item is the platform's amended-opt